016307830810546875, -0.0152435302734375, 0.016326904296875, -0.0035419464111328125, -0.0135040283203125, -0.01328277587890625, 0.01342010498046875, 0.003692626953125, 0.01482391357421875, 0.035919189453125, -0.0054779052734375, 0.01204681396484375, -0.005615234375, 0.0284576416015625, 0.0202484130859375, 0.04449462890625, -0.00043201446533203125, 0.01392364501953125, -0.0301666259765625, -0.0180511474609375, 0.0264434814453125, -0.01348114013671875, 0.0274810791015625, -0.0013837814331054688, -0.043609619140625, -0.02252197265625, 0.005245208740234375, -0.0010404586791992188, 0.01531982421875, 0.0307464599609375, -0.0133514404296875, 0.0027675628662109375, 0.0037670135498046875, 0.010162353515625, 0.0106201171875, -0.0026397705078125, 0.0038661956787109375, -0.01273345947265625, -0.022186279296875, 0.00865936279296875, -0.01186370849609375, 0.0081329345703125, 0.0012683868408203125, -0.00724029541015625, 0.039154052734375, -0.0281829833984375, -0.0186920166015625, 0.00444793701171875, 0.0174713134765625, 0.0195159912109375, -0.039642333984375, 0.0031414031982421875, 0.007091522216796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) changes how tasks are executed and raises a governance question: at what point does human direction shape the outcome? This article examines two main ways humans guide AI tasks. First, **specified delegation** refers to setting instructions, constraints, or criteria before the AI begins, clearly defining the task in advance. Second, **iterative coproduction** involves ongoing human intervention during the AI\u2019s work, where humans review, correct, or redirect outputs as they are produced.\n\nTo analyze these approaches, we use data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. The AEI aggregates monthly activity data to measure patterns of human-AI interaction across different contexts. It distinguishes between two modes of use: **1P API**, which tracks direct usage through Anthropic\u2019s application programming interface, and **Claude.ai**, which combines activity from both Chat and Cowork platforms. These modes represent different settings for human-AI collaboration, with 1P API typically reflecting more technical or integrated workflows and Claude.ai capturing a broader range of interactive uses.\n\nOur study investigates whether a stronger work-related orientation in human-AI interaction is linked to more specified delegation within each mode, and whether iterative coproduction increases more in Claude.ai than in 1P API. We focus on **level-0 O*NET tasks**, which are broad occupational activities, to provide a standardized basis for comparison. The main analysis estimates how both delegation profiles change when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal use to work-related use. Eligible node-month pairs were selected based on their presence and eligibility in both modes, resulting in 1,411 pairs for the iterative comparison.\n\nResults show that specified delegation increases by 2.76 points (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) in 1P API and by 1.45 points (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) in Claude.ai when work-related use rises. On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by \u20130.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 points in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). These findings indicate that work-related tasks are associated with clearer traces of prior human direction, especially in more technical settings. The difference between 1P API and Claude.ai suggests that the context of use shapes how and when humans intervene during AI task execution.\n\nIn summary, this article shifts attention from the quantity of AI execution to the timing and visibility of human direction. By clarifying when and how humans guide generative AI, we highlight important differences in governance and oversight across modes of use."}}